1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:03,191
Mark Twain... at death's door.

2
00:00:03,231 --> 00:00:06,018
That is what a newspaper in Washington

3
00:00:06,058 --> 00:00:08,770
printed on May thirty first, eighteen

4
00:00:08,810 --> 00:00:11,670
ninety seven. Mark Twain was not dying.

5
00:00:11,710 --> 00:00:14,422
And his answer became the most famous

6
00:00:14,462 --> 00:00:16,208
correction ever printed.

7
00:00:16,248 --> 00:00:18,439
You probably know it by heart.

8
00:00:18,479 --> 00:00:21,414
But the words you know are not the words

9
00:00:21,454 --> 00:00:22,649
he gave.

10
00:00:23,699 --> 00:00:27,049
Dying of a broken heart, in humble London

11
00:00:27,089 --> 00:00:30,109
lodgings. New York, May thirty first.

12
00:00:30,149 --> 00:00:32,672
The Evening Journal today says

13
00:00:32,712 --> 00:00:35,897
Mark Twain, the great American humorist

14
00:00:35,937 --> 00:00:39,288
and novelist, is dying in the most humble

15
00:00:39,328 --> 00:00:41,499
lodgings in London.

16
00:00:42,549 --> 00:00:44,974
Friends of the great author in this city

17
00:00:45,014 --> 00:00:47,563
say that his physical condition is brought

18
00:00:47,603 --> 00:00:49,682
about by a broken heart.

19
00:00:50,332 --> 00:00:53,306
It was not the first time that spring that

20
00:00:53,346 --> 00:00:55,171
the papers had buried him.

21
00:00:55,211 --> 00:00:57,252
And it would not be the last.

22
00:00:57,292 --> 00:00:59,907
So how does the most famous writer in

23
00:00:59,947 --> 00:01:02,491
America get reported dead, again and

24
00:01:02,531 --> 00:01:04,715
again, while he is still alive?

25
00:01:04,755 --> 00:01:06,652
And what did he really say?

26
00:01:06,692 --> 00:01:10,091
The answer is in the papers themselves.

27
00:01:10,841 --> 00:01:14,148
Part one. Dead in May.

28
00:01:15,198 --> 00:01:18,335
Earlier that month, a small paper in

29
00:01:18,375 --> 00:01:21,336
Maysville, Kentucky, printed this.

30
00:01:21,376 --> 00:01:23,102
Mark Twain not dead.

31
00:01:23,142 --> 00:01:24,779
London, May eighth.

32
00:01:24,819 --> 00:01:26,985
The report that Samuel L.

33
00:01:27,025 --> 00:01:30,339
Clemens, Mark Twain, is dead is false.

34
00:01:30,379 --> 00:01:34,598
He is in London, and in excellent health.

35
00:01:35,648 --> 00:01:38,606
In Georgia, the Savannah Morning News had

36
00:01:38,646 --> 00:01:41,458
a word for it. The canard circulated in

37
00:01:41,498 --> 00:01:43,798
the United States that Samuel L.

38
00:01:43,838 --> 00:01:46,577
Clemens was dead had not the slightest

39
00:01:46,617 --> 00:01:49,575
foundation. Mr. Clemens is in London, and

40
00:01:49,615 --> 00:01:52,427
in better health and spirits than for a

41
00:01:52,467 --> 00:01:55,352
long time past. He is still working hard

42
00:01:55,392 --> 00:01:57,162
on his new book.

43
00:01:57,812 --> 00:01:59,858
A canard is a false rumor.

44
00:01:59,898 --> 00:02:02,907
But why would anyone believe that Mark

45
00:02:02,947 --> 00:02:06,036
Twain was dying? The answer starts nine

46
00:02:06,076 --> 00:02:07,879
months earlier.

47
00:02:08,929 --> 00:02:11,064
August, eighteen ninety six.

48
00:02:11,104 --> 00:02:12,928
The same Savannah paper.

49
00:02:12,968 --> 00:02:15,336
Death of Mark Twain's daughter.

50
00:02:15,376 --> 00:02:17,045
Hartford, Connecticut.

51
00:02:17,085 --> 00:02:20,152
Olivia Susan Clemens, eldest daughter of

52
00:02:20,192 --> 00:02:23,104
Samuel L. Clemens, died tonight at her

53
00:02:23,144 --> 00:02:24,579
father's home here.

54
00:02:24,619 --> 00:02:26,773
She was twenty four.

55
00:02:27,823 --> 00:02:29,519
Her father was not there.

56
00:02:29,559 --> 00:02:32,365
While her parents and sisters were on Mr.

57
00:02:32,405 --> 00:02:35,212
Clemens's lecturing tour, she was seeking

58
00:02:35,252 --> 00:02:37,017
to regain her health here.

59
00:02:37,057 --> 00:02:40,156
Twain was abroad, on a lecture tour.

60
00:02:41,206 --> 00:02:43,960
The Associated Press later described what

61
00:02:44,000 --> 00:02:46,822
came next. Mr. Clemens was living in quiet

62
00:02:46,862 --> 00:02:49,480
lodgings in London, and working day and

63
00:02:49,520 --> 00:02:52,070
night at his book, in order to pay his

64
00:02:52,110 --> 00:02:54,591
creditors and provide for his family.

65
00:02:54,631 --> 00:02:57,317
He had reached London after the death of

66
00:02:57,357 --> 00:02:59,839
his daughter, in a terribly depressed

67
00:02:59,879 --> 00:03:02,387
condition and in bad health.

68
00:03:03,037 --> 00:03:05,801
Perfectly true at that time, the report

69
00:03:05,841 --> 00:03:08,460
said. So the story of a broken man in

70
00:03:08,500 --> 00:03:11,192
humble lodgings was built on something

71
00:03:11,232 --> 00:03:13,852
real. It was just months out of date.

72
00:03:13,892 --> 00:03:16,440
And at the end of May, it came back.

73
00:03:16,480 --> 00:03:17,511
Worse.

74
00:03:18,261 --> 00:03:21,537
Part two. Dying in poverty.

75
00:03:22,587 --> 00:03:25,063
On Monday, May thirty first, a new rumor

76
00:03:25,103 --> 00:03:26,383
went around New York.

77
00:03:26,423 --> 00:03:28,773
The next morning, the New York Journal

78
00:03:28,813 --> 00:03:31,226
described it. The rumor current in this

79
00:03:31,266 --> 00:03:33,867
city yesterday, that the well known writer

80
00:03:33,907 --> 00:03:36,520
was dying in poverty in England.

81
00:03:37,570 --> 00:03:40,810
And the Journal answered it with a cable

82
00:03:40,850 --> 00:03:43,024
from its own man in London.

83
00:03:43,064 --> 00:03:44,828
Roughing it? Nay, nay!

84
00:03:44,868 --> 00:03:48,273
Mark Twain is living in luxury, is in good

85
00:03:48,313 --> 00:03:50,979
health, and writing another book.

86
00:03:51,019 --> 00:03:54,177
Roughing It was the title of one of his

87
00:03:54,217 --> 00:03:55,637
own books.

88
00:03:56,687 --> 00:03:59,151
The same day, his friends went to the

89
00:03:59,191 --> 00:04:01,993
Associated Press. Mark Twain is all right.

90
00:04:02,033 --> 00:04:04,632
Friends deny reports of his poverty and

91
00:04:04,672 --> 00:04:07,271
ill health. And look at the date in the

92
00:04:07,311 --> 00:04:09,842
next line. They repeated the statement

93
00:04:09,882 --> 00:04:11,805
cabled on May fifth, when Mr.

94
00:04:11,845 --> 00:04:14,038
Clemens was reported to have died

95
00:04:14,078 --> 00:04:15,287
suddenly.

96
00:04:15,937 --> 00:04:18,382
Two false reports in four weeks.

97
00:04:18,422 --> 00:04:22,205
But nobody had yet asked the man himself.

98
00:04:22,955 --> 00:04:25,638
Part three. The reporter.

99
00:04:26,688 --> 00:04:28,565
Wednesday, June second.

100
00:04:28,605 --> 00:04:31,481
Front page of the New York Journal.

101
00:04:31,521 --> 00:04:34,814
Mark Twain amused. Humorist says he even

102
00:04:34,854 --> 00:04:38,231
heard on good authority that he was dead.

103
00:04:38,271 --> 00:04:40,621
Cousin, not he, sick.

104
00:04:41,671 --> 00:04:43,997
The story was by the Journal's London

105
00:04:44,037 --> 00:04:46,300
correspondent, Frank Marshall White.

106
00:04:46,340 --> 00:04:48,730
Mark Twain was undecided whether to be

107
00:04:48,770 --> 00:04:51,160
more amused or annoyed, when a Journal

108
00:04:51,200 --> 00:04:53,719
representative informed him today of the

109
00:04:53,759 --> 00:04:56,213
report in New York that he was dying in

110
00:04:56,253 --> 00:04:58,004
poverty in London.

111
00:04:59,054 --> 00:05:01,873
He is living in comfort and even luxury

112
00:05:01,913 --> 00:05:04,518
in a handsomely furnished house, in a

113
00:05:04,558 --> 00:05:07,520
beautiful square in Chelsea, with his wife

114
00:05:07,560 --> 00:05:09,089
and children.

115
00:05:10,439 --> 00:05:12,949
This is Mark Twain a few years later

116
00:05:12,989 --> 00:05:15,637
around nineteen oh two, photographed in

117
00:05:15,677 --> 00:05:18,325
his chair. He is doing what millions of

118
00:05:18,365 --> 00:05:20,255
Americans did every morning.

119
00:05:20,295 --> 00:05:22,411
Reading the newspaper.

120
00:05:23,461 --> 00:05:26,198
Then White wrote down what Twain told him.

121
00:05:26,238 --> 00:05:28,909
I can understand perfectly how the report

122
00:05:28,949 --> 00:05:30,496
of my illness got about.

123
00:05:30,536 --> 00:05:33,273
I have even heard on good authority that I

124
00:05:33,313 --> 00:05:34,508
was dead.

125
00:05:35,558 --> 00:05:38,454
James Ross Clemens, of Saint Louis, a

126
00:05:38,494 --> 00:05:41,629
cousin of mine, was seriously ill two or

127
00:05:41,669 --> 00:05:44,645
three weeks ago in London, but is well

128
00:05:44,685 --> 00:05:47,899
now. The report of my illness grew out of

129
00:05:47,939 --> 00:05:49,491
his illness.

130
00:05:50,141 --> 00:05:52,281
So that was the source.

131
00:05:52,321 --> 00:05:55,787
A different Clemens, sick in the same

132
00:05:55,827 --> 00:05:59,483
city. And then comes the line the whole

133
00:05:59,523 --> 00:06:02,208
world thinks it knows.

134
00:06:03,258 --> 00:06:05,938
The report of my death was an

135
00:06:05,978 --> 00:06:08,377
exaggeration. Eight words.

136
00:06:08,417 --> 00:06:10,347
The report, singular.

137
00:06:10,387 --> 00:06:12,316
Was. An exaggeration.

138
00:06:12,356 --> 00:06:13,988
No greatly.

139
00:06:14,638 --> 00:06:18,474
So the papers had their answer, in his own

140
00:06:18,514 --> 00:06:20,228
words. Case closed.

141
00:06:20,268 --> 00:06:24,012
Except that ten years later, the story of

142
00:06:24,052 --> 00:06:26,682
his death went to sea.

143
00:06:27,432 --> 00:06:30,643
Part four. Lost at sea.

144
00:06:31,993 --> 00:06:34,400
This is the Kanawha, a steam yacht

145
00:06:34,440 --> 00:06:37,056
photographed in eighteen ninety seven.

146
00:06:37,096 --> 00:06:38,664
It belonged to Henry H.

147
00:06:38,704 --> 00:06:39,793
Rogers.

148
00:06:41,143 --> 00:06:43,826
And this is Rogers, on the left, with

149
00:06:43,866 --> 00:06:45,960
Samuel Clemens, on the right.

150
00:06:46,000 --> 00:06:49,051
Bain News Service did not record the date.

151
00:06:49,091 --> 00:06:52,068
In the spring of nineteen oh seven, Twain

152
00:06:52,108 --> 00:06:54,916
was a guest on Rogers's yacht.

153
00:06:55,966 --> 00:06:58,875
Saturday, May fourth, nineteen oh seven.

154
00:06:58,915 --> 00:07:00,423
The New York Tribune.

155
00:07:00,463 --> 00:07:02,561
Mark Twain and yacht missing.

156
00:07:02,601 --> 00:07:05,437
Norfolk, Virginia. The wireless station

157
00:07:05,477 --> 00:07:08,460
and the weather observatory at Cape Henry

158
00:07:08,500 --> 00:07:11,261
have been appealed to, in an effort to

159
00:07:11,301 --> 00:07:14,210
locate H. H. Rogers's Kanawha, having on

160
00:07:14,250 --> 00:07:16,865
board Mark Twain. The yacht has been

161
00:07:16,905 --> 00:07:19,274
missing since Wednesday.

162
00:07:20,324 --> 00:07:23,001
Fog had trapped the yacht for days.

163
00:07:23,041 --> 00:07:26,263
Mr. Rogers and his son went north by rail.

164
00:07:26,303 --> 00:07:29,291
Mr. Clemens declined to go by rail, and

165
00:07:29,331 --> 00:07:31,620
remained on board the Kanawha.

166
00:07:31,660 --> 00:07:34,803
He fretted considerably, as the fog would

167
00:07:34,843 --> 00:07:36,142
not lift.

168
00:07:36,792 --> 00:07:39,795
A man in his seventies, on a yacht nobody

169
00:07:39,835 --> 00:07:42,615
could find, somewhere off the Virginia

170
00:07:42,655 --> 00:07:45,510
coast. If you read that over breakfast

171
00:07:45,550 --> 00:07:47,885
you would have feared the worst.

172
00:07:47,925 --> 00:07:50,974
But where was Mark Twain, really?

173
00:07:52,024 --> 00:07:54,947
The same day, in Texas, the San Antonio

174
00:07:54,987 --> 00:07:56,542
Light had the answer.

175
00:07:56,582 --> 00:07:58,593
Mark Twain not lost at all.

176
00:07:58,633 --> 00:08:00,493
The report that Samuel L.

177
00:08:00,533 --> 00:08:02,772
Clemens is missing on Henry H.

178
00:08:02,812 --> 00:08:05,735
Rogers's yacht Kanawha, which was given

179
00:08:05,775 --> 00:08:08,318
wide circulation today, is untrue.

180
00:08:08,358 --> 00:08:11,356
Mr. Clemens is at his home in this city.

181
00:08:11,396 --> 00:08:12,908
In New York.

182
00:08:14,258 --> 00:08:16,099
Twain around the same time.

183
00:08:16,139 --> 00:08:18,956
Whatever they believed in Norfolk, he was

184
00:08:18,996 --> 00:08:21,813
not on that yacht. He was in his house on

185
00:08:21,853 --> 00:08:24,252
Fifth Avenue, reading about his own

186
00:08:24,292 --> 00:08:26,761
disappearance. And, the papers said

187
00:08:26,801 --> 00:08:28,725
enjoying it hugely.

188
00:08:29,475 --> 00:08:33,018
Part five. Greatly exaggerated.

189
00:08:34,068 --> 00:08:36,896
That evening, the Washington Times ran the

190
00:08:36,936 --> 00:08:38,603
story with this headline.

191
00:08:38,643 --> 00:08:41,062
Story of his death again exaggerated

192
00:08:41,102 --> 00:08:42,248
greatly.

193
00:08:43,298 --> 00:08:45,103
Below it, the story began.

194
00:08:45,143 --> 00:08:47,656
Mark Twain had the pleasure today of

195
00:08:47,696 --> 00:08:50,565
repeating his celebrated statement, that

196
00:08:50,605 --> 00:08:53,474
the reports of my death have been greatly

197
00:08:53,514 --> 00:08:54,965
exaggerated.

198
00:08:55,615 --> 00:08:57,614
Reports, plural. Have been.

199
00:08:57,654 --> 00:08:59,124
Greatly exaggerated.

200
00:08:59,164 --> 00:09:02,069
That is not what the Journal printed in

201
00:09:02,109 --> 00:09:03,730
eighteen ninety seven.

202
00:09:03,770 --> 00:09:06,675
In ten years, his eight plain words had

203
00:09:06,715 --> 00:09:10,411
picked up a greatly, and a better rhythm.

204
00:09:11,461 --> 00:09:14,473
Right beside it, the paper printed what

205
00:09:14,513 --> 00:09:16,742
Twain actually said that day.

206
00:09:16,782 --> 00:09:18,386
Still a little foggy.

207
00:09:18,426 --> 00:09:21,672
That Actors' Fund affair is still a little

208
00:09:21,712 --> 00:09:24,020
foggy, but I'm not lost in it.

209
00:09:24,060 --> 00:09:25,820
Neither am I aboard Mr.

210
00:09:25,860 --> 00:09:29,106
Rogers's yacht. I'm simply paddling my own

211
00:09:29,146 --> 00:09:30,216
canoe.

212
00:09:30,966 --> 00:09:33,718
Printed in nineteen oh seven, as his

213
00:09:33,758 --> 00:09:35,347
celebrated statement

214
00:09:35,387 --> 00:09:38,527
the reports of my death have been greatly

215
00:09:38,567 --> 00:09:41,551
exaggerated. Printed in eighteen ninety

216
00:09:41,591 --> 00:09:43,568
seven, from his own mouth

217
00:09:43,608 --> 00:09:45,817
the report of my death was an

218
00:09:45,857 --> 00:09:48,997
exaggeration. The polished version is the

219
00:09:49,037 --> 00:09:51,033
one that survived.

220
00:09:51,783 --> 00:09:55,095
Part six. The last report.

221
00:09:56,445 --> 00:09:59,156
Bain News Service filed this photograph

222
00:09:59,196 --> 00:10:01,978
under the date April fifteenth, nineteen

223
00:10:02,018 --> 00:10:04,447
ten. Twain resting in a long chair.

224
00:10:04,487 --> 00:10:06,916
He had just come back from Bermuda.

225
00:10:06,956 --> 00:10:10,378
He was in his seventies, and he was ill.

226
00:10:11,428 --> 00:10:14,068
One week later, the Topeka State Journal.

227
00:10:14,108 --> 00:10:16,617
Mark Twain dead. Great humorist expires

228
00:10:16,657 --> 00:10:19,231
peacefully and painlessly at his country

229
00:10:19,271 --> 00:10:22,028
home among the New England hills.

230
00:10:23,078 --> 00:10:24,562
Redding, Connecticut.

231
00:10:24,602 --> 00:10:27,466
Samuel Langhorne Clemens died painlessly

232
00:10:27,506 --> 00:10:30,080
at six thirty o'clock last night, of

233
00:10:30,120 --> 00:10:33,056
angina pectoris. That was Thursday, April

234
00:10:33,096 --> 00:10:35,656
twenty first, nineteen ten.

235
00:10:36,706 --> 00:10:38,123
Give me my glasses.

236
00:10:38,163 --> 00:10:41,191
Were his last words, before lapsing into

237
00:10:41,231 --> 00:10:44,260
unconsciousness. The paper said he wrote

238
00:10:44,300 --> 00:10:46,357
them out in pencil.

239
00:10:47,007 --> 00:10:50,225
Thirteen years after the papers put him at

240
00:10:50,265 --> 00:10:53,483
death's door, the report was finally true.

241
00:10:53,523 --> 00:10:55,966
In between, they had buried him

242
00:10:56,006 --> 00:10:58,914
impoverished him, lost him at sea, and

243
00:10:58,954 --> 00:11:00,698
improved his best line.

244
00:11:00,738 --> 00:11:03,801
The improved version is the one we still

245
00:11:03,841 --> 00:11:06,982
quote. Which line do you think is better?

246
00:11:07,022 --> 00:11:09,929
The one he gave, or the one the papers

247
00:11:09,969 --> 00:11:13,207
gave him? Tell me in the comments.

248
00:11:14,357 --> 00:11:17,001
If you like stories told from the papers

249
00:11:17,041 --> 00:11:19,618
themselves, give this video a like, and

